Photocatalytic water splitting for hydrogen production has attracted tremendous attention as a green route to convert solar energy into renewable hydrogen energy and a promising technology to reduce fossil fuel consumption.[1–6] CdS has been shown to be an efficient photocatalyst due to its narrow bandgap $(2.4~\mathsf{e V})$ and suitable conduction band level.[7–9] However, its low catalytic activity resulting from rapid electron−hole recombination limit practical application of CdS.[10] Modifying CdS with cocatalysts or incorporating other materials to form solid solutions has been applied to address the above issue.[11–15] If a heterojunction can be formed by combining cocatalysts and a solid solution in one catalyst, the synergetic effects will result in efficient separation of the photogenerated electron–hole pairs.[11] However, the development of a facile synthesis method to prepare inexpensive cocatalyst/solid solution heterojunctions that possess high photocatalytic activity for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) is still challenging.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a new generation of porous crystalline materials constructed by coordination bonds between metal nodes and organic linkers.[16-19] MOFs have high compositional and structural diversities, and they have been employed as precursors for a variety of nanomaterials in the field of energy materials.[20-23] Based on their compositional and structural features, using multimetallic MOFs as precursors to create cocatalyst/solid solution heterojunctions is a promising strategy because of their homogeneous metal distribution and controllable composition. Considering the photocatalytic process, i.e. excitation by photons, photoexcited carrier transportation and reduction of $\mathsf{H}^{+}$ to $H_{2}$ ,[24,25] cocatalyst/solid solution heterojunctions derived from MOFs have the following advantages: i) the photocatalyst bandgap can be fine-tuned by altering the molar ratio of the solid solution components in the MOF precusor;[26] ii) the nanometer-sized morphology results in a shorter transfer distance for the photoexcited carriers to reach the photocatalyst surface, which further reduces the recombination of photoexcited carriers;[27-29] iii) in situ uniform loading of cocatalysts on solid solutions by uniformly metal doping the MOF precursor at the molecular level can contribute to an increase in the active sites for the reduction reaction.[30] Therefore, the synthesis of solid solution-loading cocatalysts using MOFs as precursors is highly desirable, but synthesizing catalysts with a high photocatalytic activity and long-term durability is still a challenge.
In this contribution, we introduced homogenous polymetallic MOFs as sacrificial precursors to synthesize a family of $\mathsf{N i S/Z n}_{x}\mathsf{C d}_{1-x}\mathsf{S}$ heterojunctions with NiS as the cocatalyst for the first time. These $Z n_{x}C d_{1-x}S$ solid solutions decorated with NiS cocatalyst were synthesized via $z_{n-}$ and Ni-doping a Cd-MOF, followed by solvothermal sulfidation and thermal annealing. The composite $\mathsf{N i S/Z n}_{x}\mathsf{C d}_{1-x}\mathsf{S}$ has an average size of $12\ \mathsf{n m}$ and uniform composition distribution. Importantly, by adjusting the Zn content of the $Z n_{x}C d_{1-x-M O F}$ , the chemical composition of the $Z n_{x}C d_{1-x}S$ solid solution can be fine-tuned to optimize the light absorption capacity, charge-carrier separation efficiency and photocatalytic activity by modulating the conduction band edge of the catalyst. The optimized composite NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S showed excellent photocatalytic HER activity under visible-light irradiation $(\lambda>420\mathsf{n m})$ with an extremely high stability and recyclability due to its high light absorption capacity and optimized bandgap and conduction band edge. In addition, the photocatalytic $H_{2}$ production rates of both $\mathsf{N i S}/Z\mathsf{n}_{x}\mathsf{C d}_{1-x}\mathsf{S}$ and $Z n_{x}C d_{1-x}S$ and the theoretical calculations were studied to gain insight into the mechanisms of the NiS cocatalyst in the photocatalytic HER.
We selected microsized rods of Cd-MOF $([\mathsf{C d}(\mathsf{b d c})(\mathsf{D M F})]_{\mathsf{n}}$ H2bdc $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate, DMF $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ N,Ndimethylformamide) with a pore size of $9.78\mathrm{~\AA~}\times\mathrm{~}17.07\mathrm{~\AA~}$ as the starting template because its synthesis is facile and economic and can be easily performed on a large scale (Figures S1 and S2).[31] As shown in Figure 1a, the $N i\mathrm{-}Z n_{x}C d_{1-x-M O F}$ (Figures S3 and S4, $x=0$ , 0.25, 0.5, 0.75 and 1.0) with evenly distributed ${\mathsf{N i}}^{2+}$ and $Z n^{2+}$ ions was synthesized by substituting $\mathsf{C d}^{2+}$ sites with ${\mathsf{N i}}^{2+}$ and $Z n^{2+}$ ions owing to their similar electronegativity vales (1.92 for Ni, 1.65 for $z_{n}$ and 1.69 for Cd). In the ${\mathsf{N i}}–Z{\mathsf{n}}_{x}C{\mathsf{d}}_{1-x-}{\mathsf{M O F}}$ , the optimized Ni mole percentage was $5\%$ based on the composition and HER activity analyses (Figures S5-S8 and Table S1). During the hydrothermal treatment of the ${\mathsf{N i}}–Z{\mathsf{n}}_{x}C{\mathsf{d}}_{1-x-}{\mathsf{M O F}}$ in a thioacetamide (TAA) ethanol solution, ethanol molecules were added to the $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{S}$ bond to give $C H_{3}(N H_{2})C(O C_{2}H_{5})-S H$ , which results in the formation of $\mathsf{C H}_{3}(\mathsf{N H}_{2})\mathsf{C}(\mathsf{O C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{5})_{2}$ and ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}$ .[32,33] The released ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}$ can diffuse into the channels of the $N i\mathrm{-}Z n_{x}C d_{1-x^{-}}$ MOF and react with the metal centers. Upon further thermal annealing in a ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmosphere, $\mathsf{N i S/Z n}_{x}\mathsf{C d}_{1-x}\mathsf{S}$ heterojunctions were prepared, in which NiS acts as a cocatalyst and $Z n_{x}C d_{1-x}S$ exists as the solid solution.
The elemental mapping (Figures S9-S13) and electron dispersive spectroscopy (EDS, Figure S14) results show that Ni and Zn elements were successfully doped into Cd-MOF and uniformly distributed throughout $N i{\mathrm{-}}Z n_{x}C{\mathsf{d}}_{1-x-}{\mathsf{M O F}}$ . The powder Xray diffraction (PXRD) patterns (Figure 1b) clearly depict the evolution of the $\mathsf{N i S/Z n}_{x}\mathsf{C d}_{1-x}\mathsf{S}$ crystal phase from hexagonal CdS (JCPDS Card No. 1-780) to Zn0.5Cd0.5S and, finally, cubic ZnS phase (JCPDS Card No. 1-792) with the increasing Zn content in the $\mathsf{N i S/Z n}_{x}\mathsf{C d}_{1-x}\mathsf{S}$ ( $\scriptstyle x=0$ , 0.25, 0.5, 0.75 and 1.0).
The morphologies and microstructures of the prepared samples were characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Figure S15). In addition, NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S was further characterized by high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) analysis. As shown in Figure 1c, the NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S heterojunctions are uniform nanoparticles with sizes of \~ 12 nm (Figure 1c, inset). Additionally, elemental mapping (Figure 1d) of NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S shows that all the elements (Ni, Zn, Cd, and S) are homogeneously distributed at the nanoscale over the nanoparticles. The HRTEM image of randomly selected nanoparticles shows that NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S is crystalline, as revealed by the lattice fringes (Figure 1e). The lattice spacings of 0.285 and 0.315 nm can be indexed to the (300) plane of NiS and (101) hexagonal plane of Zn0.5Cd0.5S solid solutions, respectively.[21] In addition, NiS and $Z n_{0.5}{\mathsf C}{\mathsf d}_{0.5}{\mathsf S}$ nanoparticles tightly connect with each other, which will benefit the transfer of photoexcited charge carriers in the photocatalytic reaction process.
According to X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS, Figure S16) of NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S, the peaks of Zn 2p 3/2 $\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\left(1023.9\ e\aftergroup\egroup\right)$ , Zn 2p 1/2 ( $1046.9\ \mathsf{e V})$ , Cd 3d 5/2 $(404.8~\mathsf{e V})$ and Cd 3d 3/2 (411.6 eV) are all sharp and symmetrical, demonstrating that the valences of Zn and Cd are both $+2.^{[11,34,35]}$ A weak peak is present at $857.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ due to the small amount of Ni in NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S. Two peaks at $161.2\mathsf{e V}$ and $162.4~\mathsf{e V}$ divided from S 2p peak can be attributed to the $\mathsf{S}2\mathsf{p}3/2$ and S 2p 1/2 levels, respectively.[36] The ratio of Zn and Cd in $\mathsf{N i S/Z n}_{x}\mathsf{C d}_{1-x}\mathsf{S}$ is very important for the photocatalytic performance. As shown in Table S2, the ratios of the metal elements in the final products are not consistent with those used when the MOFs were synthesized because of the different coordination abilities of the metal ions with the ligand. In addition, the EDS spectrum (Figure S17) of NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S shows that the proportion of each metal (Table S3) in $\mathsf{N i S}/Z\mathsf{n}_{0.5}\mathsf{C d}_{0.5}\mathsf{S}$ is very close to that determined by inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES).
The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas of NiS/CdS and NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S were measured by nitrogen adsorption/desorption isotherms at $77~\mathsf{K}$ (Figure S18). A sharp increase at ${\sf P}/{\sf P}_{0}$ of 0.8–1.0 indicated typical type III isotherms. Compared with NiS/CdS $(12.26~\mathsf{m}^{2}~\mathsf{g}^{-1}.$ ), the slightly enhanced surface area of NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S $(20.96~\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1};$ may be attributed to a decrease in the material density due to doping $z_{\mathsf{n}}$ into the sample, indicating that the different catalytic activities of NiS/CdS and $\mathsf{N i S}/Z\mathsf{n}_{0.5}\mathsf{C d}_{0.5}\mathsf{S}$ will not be caused by changes in the specific surface area.
The UV/Vis diffuse reflectance spectra of $\mathsf{N i S}/Z\mathsf{n}_{x}\mathsf{C d}_{1-x}\mathsf{S}$ were measured to confirm the light-harvesting abilities. As shown in Figure 2a, the intensities of visible-light absorption vary with the CdS content in $\mathsf{N i S}/Z\mathsf{n}_{x}\mathsf{C d}_{1-x}\mathsf{S}$ , indicating that the light absorption capacity of the material can be tuned by changing the amount of CdS. The corresponding bandgap energies (Figure S19) were estimated from the UV−visible diffuse absorption spectra by the Kubelka−Munk method using the following equation:
$$
\alpha h v=\mathsf{A}(h v-E_{\mathsf{g}})^{1/2}
$$
where $\boldsymbol{a}$ is the absorption coefficient, hν is the photon energy, ${\cal E}_{9}$ is the direct bandgap, and A is a constant.[37,38] The calculated bandgap energy increased from 2.30 to $3.01~\mathsf{e V}$ as the $x$ value increased from 0 to 1.0 (Figure 2b). This trend is consistent with the calculated density functional theory (DFT) results,[26] showing that the incorporation of $Z n^{2+}$ ions in CdS can widen the bandgap and narrow the absorption range of CdS to the visible-light region. The solid solutions possess the capacity to harness visible-light absorption and regulate the reducing capacity by controlling the composition, proving the coordination between the absorption and band engineering. For $\mathsf{N i S/Z n}_{x}\mathsf{C d}_{1-x}\mathsf{S}$ , NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S exhibits the smallest Nyquist plot diameter in the EIS (Figure 2c), indicating its low charge-transfer resistance.[39,40] Moreover, the photocurrent density of $\mathsf{N i S}/Z\mathsf{n}_{0.5}\mathsf{C d}_{0.5}\mathsf{S}$ is the highest (Figure 2d), suggesting highly efficient separation of photoexcited charge carriers.[41] This separation is attributed to its suitable bandgap as a result of the optimal proportion of ZnS and CdS in NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S.
We further investigated the photocatalytic activity of $\mathsf{N i S/Z n}_{x}\mathsf{C d}_{1-x}\mathsf{S}$ heterojunctions for the production of hydrogen in the presence of $N a_{2}S$ and $N a_{2}S O_{3}$ as sacrificial electron donors without the use of cocatalysts under visible light $(>420\ \mathsf{n m},$ Figure S20). As shown in Figure 3a, the HER rate of NiS/ZnS under visible-light irradiation is negligible (0.09 mmol ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}\ {\mathfrak{h}}^{-1})$ . Upon increasing the amount of CdS in $\mathsf{N i S/Z n}_{x}\mathsf{C d}_{1-x}\mathsf{S}$ , the HER rate of $\mathsf{N i S/Z n}_{x}\mathsf{C d}_{1-x}\mathsf{S}$ gradually increases and reaches a high value of $16.78\ m m o l\ g^{-1}\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ for NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S, which is 186 times greater than the HER rate of $N i S/Z n S$ . The HER rate then decreases upon further increasing the CdS content. For NiS/CdS, the hydrogen production rate decreases to $3.52\ m m o l\ g^{-1}\ h^{-1}$ . The light-harvesting ability and conduction band edge of a catalyst are crucial and affect the photocatalytic performance.[26] For example, pure ZnS has a good ability to reduce hydrogen, but it does not absorb visible light owing to its large bandgap. Although CdS has a suitable bandgap to absorb visible light, its lower conduction band position results in its weak hydrogen production capacity. For NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S, the light absorption capacity of the catalyst and edge of the conduction band achieve a good balance due to from the relative contents of ZnS and CdS in ${\sf N i S}/{Z{\sf n}_{x}{\sf C d}_{1-x}{\sf S}},$ which is also the main reason why NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S possesses the highest photocatalytic performance of the NiS/ZnxCd1-xS heterojunctions.
Nickel sulfide, composed of two earth-abundant element, has been identified as promising cocatalysts for photocatalytic hydrogen evolution.[42] To highlight the importance of NiS in the photocatalytic performance, NiS and various solid solutions $Z n_{x}C d_{1-x}S$ $(x=0$ , 0.25, 0.5, 0.75 and 1.0, Figure S22) were synthesized by calcining Ni-MOF and $Z n_{x}C d_{1-x}-M O F$ under the same conditions. The photocatalytic HER rates of NiS and $Z n_{x}C d_{1-x}S$ (Figure S23) are much lower than that of $\mathsf{N i S}/Z\mathsf{n}_{x}\mathsf{C d}_{1-x}\mathsf{S}$ This result indicates that the cocatalyst NiS plays an important role in enhancing the photocatalytic activity.
In addition to the experimental results mentioned above, we further applied DFT-based first-principle calculations to study the relationship between the NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S heterostructure and photocatalytic activity and to confirm the importance of the NiS cocatalyst in increasing the photocatalytic activity of solid solution $Z n_{x}C d_{1-x}S$ . In the charge transport analysis, the potential is higher for $Z n_{0.5}{\mathsf C}{\mathsf d}_{0.5}{\mathsf S}$ and lower in the NiS zone (Figure 4a), which allows the photogenerated charge to be transferred from Zn0.5Cd0.5S to NiS, providing an electron to reduce adsorbed hydrogen. The band structure of Zn0.5Cd0.5S is depicted in Figure 4b, and the model used for this calculation is given in Figure S24. Zn0.5Cd0.5S has a semiconductor character with a direct bandgap of $1.23\:\mathrm{eV}$ , which is consistent with previous results by Li et al.[26] As the band structure depicts, the valence band maximum and conduction band minimum are both located at the gamma point. The DFT-derived bandgap can be used only for a qualitative analysis because the DFT method inevitably underestimates the bandgap of semiconductors. The band structure of Zn0.5Cd0.5S shows that the valence band has more contribution from S, while metal atoms mainly contribute to the conduction band. The bright color of the valence band shows that Zn contributes more than Cd, while the dark color of the conduction band shows that Cd is prominent in the unoccupied sates.
The stability of NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S was tested by repeating the experiments under identical conditions and measuring the HER rate for each cycle, as shown in Figure 3b. After 20 h of irradiation, the $H_{2}$ production rates of $\mathsf{N i S}/Z\mathsf{n}_{0.5}\mathsf{C d}_{0.5}\mathsf{S}$ still remained the same after five cycles. In addition, the recovered catalyst was characterized by PXRD (Figure S21a) and TEM (Figures S21b and S21c), which confirmed that the basic structure framework and morphology of NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S were preserved after cycling. All of the above results demonstrate the
Furthermore, we investigated the water dissociation reaction barrier with different models, as shown in Figure 4c. NiS is a wellknown active electrocatalyst for the HER owing to its lower barrier for the water dissociation reaction, which is the most important step of the HER in alkaline media.[43] In this case, we found that when combined with Zn0.5Cd0.5S, the catalytic activity of NiS to dissociate $H_{2}O$ into $^{\star}\mathsf{H}$ and $^{\star}\mathsf{O}\mathsf{H}$ is highly promoted. The barrier was calculated to be 1.14, 0.75 and $0.52\ {\tt e V}$ for the Zn0.5Cd0.5S, NiS and NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S models, respectively (optimized geometry structures are shown in Figure S25). Owing to the electronic effect of Zn0.5Cd0.5S, the affinity of NiS for OH is enhanced, which is consistent with the projected density of states (Figure 4d). The band center of Ni shifts closer to the Fermi level, leading to stronger adsorption of OH, which decreases the barrier of the water dissociation reaction (Figure S26). In this regard, more surface hydrogen is provided for reduction by the charge transported from Zn0.5Cd0.5S. Thus, the faster water dissociation kinetics of NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S is the key to its higher photocatalytic hydrogen evolution activity than that of $Z n_{0.5}{\mathsf C}{\mathsf d}_{0.5}{\mathsf S}$ .
In summary, a metal-organic framework (MOF)-template strategy with solvothermal sulfidation and thermal annealing was successfully developed to fabricate $\mathsf{N i S/Z n}_{x}\mathsf{C d}_{1-x}\mathsf{S}$ heterojunctions for photocatalytic $H_{2}$ production. The prepared heterojunctions have homogeneous composition distributions owing to uniform sulfidation of the MOF precursor at a molecular level. By adjusting the doping metal concentration in the MOFs, the chemical compositions of the polymetallic sulfide can be finetuned, and the light absorption capacity and photocatalytic activity were significantly enhanced. NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S exhibits an optimal HER rate of 16.78 mmol $\mathfrak{g}^{-1}\ \mathfrak{h}^{-1}$ and a high stability and recyclability under visible-light irradiation $(\lambda>420\ \mathsf{n m})$ , which can be attributed to its high light absorption capacity, appropriate bandgap and position of the conduction band edge. This work not only demonstrates a facile method to produce highly active NiS/Zn0.5Cd0.5S for photocatalytic HER but also provides deep insight into regulating the photocatalytic activity of $\mathsf{N i S/Z n}_{x}\mathsf{C d}_{1-x}\mathsf{S}$ heterojunctions for the design of new, advanced catalysts.
